# 15. NGINX Ingress

The Minikube Ingress addon was enabled:

```bash
minikube addons enable ingress -p assignment1
```

The NGINX Ingress controller was verified:

```bash
kubectl get pods -n ingress-nginx
```

The controller was running successfully.

---

# 16. Ingress Configuration

The application Ingress is defined in:

```text
k8s/app-ingress.yaml
```

The hostname is:

```text
myapp.example.com
```

Routing rules:

```text
/api  → backend-service:3000
/     → frontend-service:80
```

The Ingress therefore provides one external entry point for both application components.

---

# 17. Ingress Security / RBAC

A Kubernetes ServiceAccount was created:

```bash
kubectl create serviceaccount platform-team -n assignment1
```

A Role was created to allow controlled modification of Ingress resources:

```bash
kubectl create role ingress-editor \
  --verb=get,list,watch,update,patch \
  --resource=ingresses \
  -n assignment1
```

A RoleBinding connected the ServiceAccount to the Role:

```bash
kubectl create rolebinding platform-team-can-edit-ingress \
  --role=ingress-editor \
  --serviceaccount=assignment1:platform-team \
  -n assignment1
```

Permission was tested:

```bash
kubectl auth can-i update ingresses \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:assignment1:platform-team \
  -n assignment1
```

Result:

```text
yes
```

The default ServiceAccount was also tested and did not have the same permission.

This demonstrates the principle of **least privilege**: access should be granted only to the resources and operations that are required.

---

# 18. Ingress Controller Security Configuration

The NGINX Ingress controller ConfigMap was configured with:

```yaml
allow-snippet-annotations: "false"
```

This prevents arbitrary NGINX configuration snippets from being injected through Ingress annotations.

The controller was restarted:

```bash
kubectl rollout restart deployment/ingress-nginx-controller \
  -n ingress-nginx
```

The rollout was verified with:

```bash
kubectl rollout status deployment/ingress-nginx-controller \
  -n ingress-nginx
```

---

# 19. cert-manager Installation

cert-manager was installed using Helm.

The Jetstack repository was added:

```bash
helm repo add jetstack https://charts.jetstack.io
```

Then:

```bash
helm repo update
```

The available cert-manager versions were checked:

```bash
helm search repo jetstack/cert-manager --versions | head -10
```

Version `v1.21.2` was selected.

Installation:

```bash
helm install cert-manager jetstack/cert-manager \
  --namespace cert-manager \
  --create-namespace \
  --version v1.21.2 \
  --set crds.enabled=true
```

The cert-manager components were verified:

```bash
kubectl get pods -n cert-manager
```

The following components were running:

```text
cert-manager
cert-manager-cainjector
cert-manager-webhook
```

---

# 20. Self-Signed ClusterIssuer

A ClusterIssuer was created in:

```text
k8s/cluster-issuer.yaml
```

```yaml
apiVersion: cert-manager.io/v1
kind: ClusterIssuer
metadata:
  name: selfsigned-issuer
spec:
  selfSigned: {}
```

It was applied with:

```bash
kubectl apply -f k8s/cluster-issuer.yaml
```

Verification:

```bash
kubectl get clusterissuer
```

Result:

```text
selfsigned-issuer   True
```

---

# 21. TLS Configuration

The Ingress was updated to use the self-signed issuer:

```yaml
annotations:
  cert-manager.io/cluster-issuer: selfsigned-issuer
  nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/ssl-redirect: "true"
```

TLS configuration:

```yaml
tls:
  - hosts:
      - myapp.example.com
    secretName: app-tls-secret
```

The change from:

```yaml
ssl-redirect: "false"
```

to:

```yaml
ssl-redirect: "true"
```

was made because HTTPS was now configured.

This means HTTP requests are redirected to HTTPS.

---

# 22. Certificate Verification

The generated TLS Secret was checked:

```bash
kubectl get secret app-tls-secret -n assignment1
```

Result:

```text
app-tls-secret   kubernetes.io/tls
```

The certificate was also checked:

```bash
kubectl get certificate -n assignment1
```

Result:

```text
NAME             READY
app-tls-secret   True
```

The certificate details showed:

```text
DNS Name:
myapp.example.com

Issuer:
selfsigned-issuer

Status:
True
```

This confirmed that cert-manager successfully generated the certificate.

---

# 23. HTTPS Testing

The Minikube IP was:

```text
192.168.76.2
```

HTTPS frontend testing was performed with:

```bash
curl -k --resolve myapp.example.com:443:192.168.76.2 \
  https://myapp.example.com/
```

The response contained:

```html
<h1>Welcome to Our Store</h1>
```

The backend was tested with:

```bash
curl -k --resolve myapp.example.com:443:192.168.76.2 \
  https://myapp.example.com/api
```

The response was:

```json
{
  "message": "Backend is running",
  "dbHost": "mongodb-service",
  "dbUser": "admin"
}
```

The `-k` option was required because the certificate is self-signed and therefore is not trusted by the normal system certificate authority.

---

# 24. HTTP to HTTPS Redirect

The redirect was tested with:

```bash
curl -I http://myapp.example.com
```

The result was:

```text
HTTP/1.1 308 Permanent Redirect
Location: https://myapp.example.com
```

This confirms that HTTP traffic is automatically redirected to HTTPS.

---

# 25. Local Hostname Configuration

Because `myapp.example.com` is not a real DNS record pointing to the local Minikube cluster, the local Ubuntu `/etc/hosts` file was updated.

The following entry was added:

```text
192.168.76.2 myapp.example.com
```

It was verified with:

```bash
getent hosts myapp.example.com
```

Result:

```text
192.168.76.2    myapp.example.com
```

This allows the local computer to resolve:

```text
myapp.example.com
```

to the Minikube IP.

---

